      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we need to determine the smallest positive value \\( T \\) such that \\( y(x + T) = y(x) \\) for all \\( x \\). Let's start by expressing the function in terms of sine and cosine.\n\nThe tangent function can be written as:\n\\[ \\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\]\nThe cotangent function can be written as:\n\\[ \\cot x = \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\]\nThus, the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) becomes:\n\\[ y = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} \\]\nTo combine these terms, we find a common denominator:\n\\[ y = \\frac{\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x}{\\sin x \\cos x} \\]\nSince \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\), we have:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x} \\]\nWe know that:\n\\[ \\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x \\]\nThus, the function can be rewritten as:\n\\[ y = \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x} = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x} \\]\nThe function \\( y = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x} \\) is periodic with the same period as \\( \\sin 2x \\). The function \\( \\sin 2x \\) has a period of \\( \\pi \\) because the period of \\( \\sin kx \\) is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{k} \\). Therefore, the period of \\( \\sin 2x \\) is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\). Since the fraction \\( \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x} \\) will have the same period as \\( \\sin 2x \\), the period of \\( y = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x} \\) is also \\( \\pi \\).\n\nThus, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\pi} \\]",
