      "text": "Given $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$, we can rewrite $\\tan x + \\cot x$ as $\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} = \\frac{\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x}{\\sin x \\cos x}$. This simplifies using the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$ to $\\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x}$.\n\nRecall the double angle identity $\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x$, which gives $\\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\sin x \\cos x} = 2 \\csc 2x$. \n\nTherefore, $y = \\frac{1}{\\sin x \\cos x} = 2 \\csc 2x$.\n\nWe know that $\\csc 2x = \\frac{1}{\\sin 2x}$. Thus, $y = 2 \\csc 2x = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin 2x} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\sin x \\cos x}$.\n\nWe can further simplify this to $y = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\sin x \\cos x} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\sin x \\cos x} = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\sin x \\cos x} = \\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}$.\n\nFinally, to find the period of $y$, we consider the period of $\\sin 2x$. The sine function $\\sin x$ has a period of $2\\pi$. \n\nFor $\\sin 2x$, the argument is doubled, so its period is compressed, and thus it will have a period of $\\frac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi$.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$ is \\boxed{\\pi}.",
