      "text": "First, we simplify the function $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$. Recall that $\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}$. Thus, we have:\n\\[\ny = \\tan x + \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\n\\]\n\nLet's denote $\\tan x$ as $t$. Then the function becomes:\n\\[\ny = t + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\n\nTo find the period of $y = t + \\frac{1}{t}$, we need to determine the smallest positive value $P$ such that:\n\\[\nt + \\frac{1}{t} = t + \\frac{1}{t + P}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{t} = \\frac{1}{t + P}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\nt = t + P \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t^2 = t(P + 1)\n\\]\n\nSolving the second equation:\n\\[\nt^2 = tp + t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t^2 - tp - t = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t(t - p - 1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince $t \\neq 0$, we have:\n\\[\nt = p + 1\n\\]\n\nThus, $P + 1 = t$ implies:\n\\[\nP = t - 1\n\\]\n\nSince $t = \\tan x$, and the period of $\\tan x$ is $\\pi$, we have:\n\\[\nP = \\pi - 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the period of the function $y = \\tan x + \\cot x$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi - 1}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, let's use Python code to confirm the periodicity by evaluating the function at several points within the interval $[0, \\pi - 1)$ and ensuring it repeats every $\\pi - 1$.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the period P\nP = np.pi - 1\n\n# Define the function y = tan(x) + cot(x)\ndef y(x):\n    return np.tan(x) + 1/np.tan(x)\n\n# Evaluate the function at several points within one period\npoints = np.linspace(0, P, 1000)\nevaluations = y(points)\n\n# Check if the evaluations repeat every P\ncritical_points = np.unique(np.round(evaluations, decimals=5))\nis_periodic = np.allclose(evaluations, critical_points[1:] - critical_points[:-1])\n\nprint(((P, is_periodic)))\n```",
