      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the period of the function \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\), we start by expressing the function in a more convenient form. Recall that \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\). Thus, we can rewrite the function as:\n\\[\ny = \\tan x + \\frac{1}{\\tan x}.\n\\]\nLet \\( t = \\tan x \\). Then the function becomes:\n\\[\ny = t + \\frac{1}{t}.\n\\]\nWe need to determine the period of the function \\( y = t + \\frac{1}{t} \\). The period of the function \\( t \\) is \\( \\pi \\) because \\(\\tan (x + \\pi) = \\tan x\\). Therefore, the period of \\( \\frac{1}{t} \\) is also \\( \\pi \\) because \\(\\frac{1}{\\tan (x + \\pi)} = \\frac{1}{\\tan x} = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\). Since the sum of two periodic functions with the same period will also have that period, the function \\( y = t + \\frac{1}{t} \\) is periodic with period \\( \\pi \\).\n\nTherefore, the period of \\( y = \\tan x + \\cot x \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\pi}\\).",
